== Magnetic Field Imaging
<magnetic-field-imaging>
To demonstrate the capability of imaging spatiotemporal magnetic fields,
we positioned a bipolar platinum--iridium electrode with two 50~μm tips
above the NV layer in a 1~mmol~L#super[−1] NaCl solution prepared in
Milli-Q water. The optimal microwave (MW) frequency for sensing is
selected by computing the spatial average of the ODMR spectra across all
pixels and identifying the frequency with the steepest slope averaged
across all pixels. This frequency is then used for subsequent sensing
measurements.

A time-dependent voltage is applied with a DAC card (National
Instruments), delivering 100~ms, 1~V square pulse. The current
stimulation pulses are synchronized with the laser scanning via a
digital marker output from the AWG. The magnetic field induced by the
current pulse across the electrodes is mapped in real time using our
scanning NV magnetometry technique. For this experiment, a dwell time of
$tau_d =$ 1.2~μs was chosen with 21 $times$ 21 pixels, resulting in a
frame rate of 2~kHz. The scanned region covers
170 $times$ 170~μm#super[2], with a Gaussian beam spot size of 10~μm and
a center-to-center pixel spacing of 8~μm yielding an effective overlap
of $approx$15~% between adjacent pixels. This degree of overlap provides
sub-beam sampling and is consistent with the Nyquist criteria used in
raster-scanned imaging to avoid undersampling and reconstruction
artifacts~@pawley2006handbook.

To suppress pixel-to-pixel amplitude variations arising from spatial
differences in optical collection efficiency, NV density, and local
microwave detuning, each time trace was normalized to the response
generated by a weak externally applied sinusoidal magnetic field at
527~Hz. This calibration field was produced by a pair of coils
positioned sufficiently far from the sensing region so that the
resulting magnetic field can be considered homogeneous across the entire
scanned area. The procedure equalizes the relative signal scaling
between pixels but does not alter the intrinsic magnetic sensitivity,
which remains limited by the local noise level at each pixel.

= Results
<results>
== Quasi-Continuous Wave ODMR
<quasi-continuous-wave-odmr>
Our magnetic field imaging protocol combines continuous wave microwave
excitation with spatially and temporally localized optical excitation of
NV centers. The temporal spacing between successive optical excitation
pulses is set by the scan time per frame, while the pulse duration is
fixed by the pixel dwell time. This excitation scheme shares conceptual
similarities with the pulsed-ODMR method introduced by Dréau et
al.~@dreau2011avoiding, where optical read out pulses applied in the
dark suppress power broadening and recover the inhomogeneous linewidth.
In contrast, our microwaves are left continuously on, and the optical
pulses arise naturally from the beam-scanning dwell time rather than
from explicit pulse gating, producing a quasi-CW regime in which
coherent evolution and relaxation occur between successive optical
resets. To characterize the performance of the protocol, we extract the
ODMR spectrum at one pixel and determine the magnetic-field sensitivity
from the ODMR contrast and linewidth as a function of the scan time. We
would like to emphasize that in our scanning configuration with a fixed
pixel dwell time $tau_d$, the total number of pixels per frame directly
determines the pulse spacing time. Thus, increasing the image size or
spatial resolution (number of pixels) inherently increases the pulse
spacing time for each pixel, affecting the ODMR readout properties. The
results are summarized in @fig:figure2 a) and b).

With zero pulse spacing, both linewidth and contrast are determined by
the steady state conditions in the continuous wave (CW)
regime~@webb2020optimization@ahmadi2017pump. For a fixed dwell time of
0.4~μs and with increasing pulse spacing, we observe an overall increase
of contrast as compared to the CW regime. A maximum occurs for a pulse
spacing corresponding to a nominal $pi$-pulse of the spin Rabi frequency
$Omega_R$. We tested values of $Omega_R$ of 70~kHz and 140~kHz,
respectively, while the optical excitation rate is
$Gamma_p approx 1 med upright("M") upright("Hz")$ under our illumination
conditions. The optical pulse effectively polarizes the NV centers into
the bright $m_s = 0$ spin-state, while between two pulses the spin
population is coherently driven by the continuously applied microwave
field. However, the maximum contrast does not occur at the $pi$-point of
the corresponding Rabi-frequency, but instead appears at a significantly
longer pulse spacing time around 100~μs in our case. We associate this
observation of a delayed contrast peak with inhomogeneous broadening of
the ensemble NV center spin transitions larger than the applied
$Omega_R$, reducing the efficiency of coherent population transfer and
shifting the timing of maximum readout contrast. This interpretation is
supported by our numerical simulations (see Supplementary Materials),
which reproduce the delayed contrast maximum and show that microwave
detuning across the ensemble modifies the effective rotation rate and
damps the oscillation envelope.

As a function of optical pulse spacing, the linewidths (full width at
half maximum) initially become narrower than in the CW limit, then
broaden with increasing pulse spacing. This observation reflects a
transition from coherent to relaxation-dominated dynamics.
@fig:figure2\(c) compares the ODMR spectrum of a single pixel
acquired under CW and qCW conditions with a Rabi frequency of
$Omega_R = 70 med upright("k") upright("Hz")$. The qCW spectrum, taken
at a pulse spacing of 100~μs, exhibits enhanced contrast consistent with
the dynamics described above.

We use a 5-level model~@ahmadi2017pump@robledo2011spin to simulate the
evolution and readout of the spin states. This model qualitatively
captures the key features observed in our experiments, including the
position of the $pi$-pulse peak and the subsequent contrast maximum (see
Supplementary). This agreement supports the interpretation that compared
to the CW-regime, contrast enhancement arises from coherent spin
dynamics related to the qCW regime applied. At longer pulse spacings,
the contrast decreases as longitudinal spin relaxation reduces net
polarization before the next readout, indicating a spin-lifetime
$T_1$-limited performance.

To quantify the magnetic sensing performance, we extract noise floor
levels from 1~s time-traces as a function of pulse spacing
(@fig:figure3\(a)). The observed $sqrt(t)$ scaling as a function of
pulse spacing reflects the reduction in collected photons at longer
spacings over a fixed total measurement time, with the CW limit
providing a baseline for comparison.

The corresponding sensitivity trends are summarized in
@fig:figure3\(b). The solid purple curve indicates the expected
scaling with fixed contrast and linewidth obtained from CW reference
measurements and accounting for reduction in collected photons with
$sqrt(t)$-scaling, while the dashed line marks the attainable
sensitivity of the single pixel in the CW regime. For short pulse
spacings, our qCW data initially outperforms the idealized CW behavior
(constant linewidth and contrast), benefiting from enhanced contrast,
but ultimately degrades at longer spacings due to reduced photon counts
and decreased spin-polarization. These results highlight the trade-offs
between coherent contrast enhancement and relaxation-induced sensitivity
loss in the qCW-regime.

