<fig:figure4>

Using the electrode stimulation setup described above, we recorded
time-resolved maps of the magnetic field projection along the effective
sensing axis, $B_(\[ 110 \])$, during the application of a 1~V, 100~ms
square pulse across the bipolar Pt--Ir electrode in 1~mmol~L#super[−1]
NaCl. The magnetic field was imaged using our scanning magnetometry
technique with a mean pixel sensitivity of 10~nT/√Hz in the
100~Hz--1000~Hz band and averaging time of 20~min. Reconstructing the
time traces from individual pixels enables direct visualization of the
spatiotemporal evolution of $B_(\[ 110 \])$ throughout the sensing
region, as shown in @fig:figure4. The microscope image in
@fig:figure4\(c) indicates the placement of the bipolar electrode
relative to the $tilde.op 170 times 170 med upright("μ") upright("m")^2$
scanned area.

Representative frames of $B_(\[ 110 \])$ (@fig:figure4\(a)) reveal two
localized regions of opposite sign beneath the two electrode tips in
this projection, while the signal in the region between the electrodes
remains close to zero within our noise floor. Pixel-resolved time traces
(@fig:figure4\(b)) show the onset of $B_(\[ 110 \])$ that is
synchronous with the applied 100~ms voltage pulse, followed by a slow
decay that extends well beyond the duration of the stimulation.
Throughout the pulse, the antisymmetric polarity of $B_(\[ 110 \])$
under the two electrodes is preserved. As shown in the Supplementary
Material, repeating the experiment with inverted polarity of the
stimulation pulse results in a corresponding inversion of the two
opposite-phase regions, confirming the expected sign reversal of
$B_(\[ 110 \])$. We also note that the spatially averaged trace (red
curve in @fig:figure4\(b)) remains close to zero throughout the
stimulation, as the opposite-sign contributions from the two electrode
regions cancel when integrated over the field of view. This highlights
the importance of spatially resolved magnetic imaging, since such
antisymmetric field patterns would be strongly suppressed in
measurements relying solely on non-spatially resolved detection.

The experiment is operated below the onset of visible Faradaic
reactions: no gas evolution is observed at 1~V in 1~mmol~L#super[−1]
NaCl, indicating that we are in a sub-catalytic, predominantly
capacitive regime. In this limit, the dominant response arises from
charging of the electrode--electrolyte interface and the subsequent
redistribution of ions in the bulk solution, rather than sustained
electrolysis. The pronounced long-lived decay of $B_(\[ 110 \])$ after
the end of the voltage pulse, on the order of hundreds of milliseconds,
is consistent with relaxation of ionic concentration gradients
established during the transient polarization of the two electrode tips,
which drive a slowly decaying magnetic field signal. Taking a diffusion
coefficient $D = 1 times 10^(- 9) thin upright(m)^2 \/ upright(s)$ for
monovalent ions in water and a characteristic transport length of
$L tilde.op 10 med upright("μ") upright("m")$, corresponding to the
pixel size in our measurements, one obtains a diffusion time
$tau_D = L^2 \/ D approx 0.1 med upright("s")$. This timescale agrees
with the hundreds of milliseconds relaxation observed in the measured
magnetic field following the stimulation pulse.

= Discussion
<discussion>
We have demonstrated a scanning NV magnetometry technique based on
acousto-optic beam deflection and quasi-continuous wave ODMR (qCW-ODMR),
enabling high-speed, high-sensitivity magnetic imaging with ensembles of
NV centers. In addition, common mode rejection (CMR) is implemented in
software to suppress laser intensity fluctuations, further reducing
technical noise and improving signal quality.

By combining continuous microwave excitation with spatially localized,
pulsed optical readout and repolarization, the system enables spin-state
manipulation without requiring precise microwave timing
control---eliminating the need for expensive arbitrary waveform
generators or gated microwave pulses. Instead, the spin dynamics are
tuned via the inter-pulse spacing set by the scan configuration. This
intermediate excitation regime exhibits a well-defined transition from
coherent spin evolution to $T_1$ relaxation-limited behavior, as
confirmed by experimental measurements and numerical modeling of
contrast and linewidth across pulse spacing (see supplementary
information).

The qCW-scheme enables flexible control over the trade-off between
sensitivity and frame rate. As the number of pixels increases, the
spacing between consecutive pulses per pixel grows, leading to higher
noise floors and reduced sensitivity due to decreased photon counts per
pixel (@fig:figure3\(b)). Nonetheless, for moderate pulse spacings,
the technique achieves sensitivities comparable to the continuous-wave
limit while offering adaptable spatial sampling.

To demonstrate the utility of this platform, we imaged the
spatiotemporal evolution of magnetic fields generated by pulsed currents
through a microelectrode in a 1~mmol~L#super[−1] NaCl solution. The
system resolved both the amplitude and the phase of the induced magnetic
field (@fig:figure4). Notably, we observed long-lived magnetic
tails persisting after the 100~ms current stimulation pulse. This
prolonged signal is attributed to diffusive ionic currents in the
solution with timescales consistent with ion diffusion over tens of
microns.

The scanning approach inherently introduces a trade-off between spatial
resolution and temporal bandwidth, since increasing the number of pixels
reduces the effective frame rate for a fixed dwell time. In our
configuration, this also increases the inter-pulse spacing at each
voxel, which directly alters the local spin dynamics. As characterized
in @fig:figure2, this modifies both ODMR contrast and linewidth,
directly affecting the magnetic sensitivity.

In addition, inhomogeneities in strain, microwave field amplitude, and
optical intensity contribute to spatial variation in ODMR spectrum and
sensitivity to magnetic field. These effects arise in part because all
NV centers in the field of view are addressed using a single global
microwave frequency, which cannot be simultaneously resonant across the
entire inhomogeneously broadened ensemble. We partially mitigate this
limitation by selecting the drive frequency based on spatially averaged
ODMR spectra, though local detuning remains a limiting factor for
uniform contrast and sensitivity. Additionally, we correct
pixel-to-pixel gain variations by normalizing each trace to the response
from a homogeneous 527 Hz calibration field generated by two external
coils placed far from the sensing region.

To contextualize the performance of our approach, we compared the
sensitivity and frame rate of the AOD-scanning system against a range of
state-of-the-art scientific and high-speed cameras commonly used for NV
magnetometry (@fig:figure5\(a)). Sensitivities were normalized by
pixel area and field of view to enable a fair comparison across all
imaging sensors. Our system outperforms conventional cameras by
achieving superior sensitivity at significantly higher frame rates,
owing to its high dynamic range, fast beam steering, and optimized
readout strategy. A detailed description of the calculation procedure is
provided in the Supplementary Materials. @fig:figure5\(b) further
illustrates the difference in performance by comparing the projected
magnetic noise as a function of averaging time for our AOD system and a
high-end lock-in camera (heliCam C4). To reach a signal-to-noise ratio
(SNR) of 3 for a 1~nT magnetic signal, representative for weak
biological fields, the AOD system requires approximately 2.5~h of
averaging, while the heliCam would require over 40~h under ideal
conditions. Such extended acquisition times are incompatible with
biological measurements, since tissue viability typically cannot be
maintained beyond $tilde.op$24~h. These results highlight the practical
advantage of the AOD approach for detecting weak magnetic signals in
living tissue within realistic experimental time frames. \

